From f1e643f9894ede7647aa4b787ec3a7d58244b562 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:40:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVBridge: the content mapping v4.0 as a setting
 (videoscreen.hadesdvcm40)

Until now the default v4.0 part of dovi_tool could be added to RPUs
without one only with <dvcm40default> in advancedsettings.xml. People
who come from CoreELEC and its builds look for it in the settings
(the "append CMv4.0" of CoreELEC p3i, avdvplus and SamuriHL's build,
whose settings and notes were read as data; SamuriHL's "decide once per
movie" is the reason the automatic choice is made once a stream), and
some TVs of display management 4 map a master of 4000 cd/m2 without
level 2 trims better with it. The other way, a TV that shows a black
or wrong picture with the v4.0 part needs it left out.

System > Display > "Dolby Vision content mapping v4.0" (expert level):
 - As in the video: the metadata as the colourist made them, the
   default and what native output did before;
 - Add v4.0 where the video has none: dovi_tool's default part (levels
   3 with no offsets, 9 DCI-P3 D65, 11 cinema and 254 of CM v4.0.2; no
   level 8 trims, no level 10), as <dvcm40default> did;
 - Automatic, once per video: the same, decided with the first frame
   made of a stream: added where its RPU has no v4.0 part and its
   mastering display (source_max_pq) is brighter than the peak of the
   display from its Dolby VSVDB (never where that is not known);
 - Version 2.9 only: the v4.0 part left out for any display, as for a
   display of display management 2 or 3.
It changes what the TV does with the picture, which is no longer what
the master was graded for: off by default, and the help says so.
<dvcm40default>true</dvcm40default> still adds it, whatever the setting.

The player information shows it in "Content mapping sent": "v2.9 + v4.0
added (the setting)", "(automatic: mastered brighter than the TV)" or
"v2.9 (the setting)"; kodi.log says it when native output starts and
in the line of the first picture of a stream. English and Spanish.
